# What the Docker build context must NOT contain.
#
# The build context is everything sent to the daemon before a single line of the
# Dockerfile runs. On this repository that would otherwise include a `.venv/`
# with 200+ MB of Windows wheels, `web/node_modules/` with the full Next.js
# dependency tree, and several SQLite databases left by local test runs -- none
# of which the image needs, all of which slow every build and can leak local
# state into an image. The rules below are the same set `.gitignore` uses where
# the reasons coincide, plus the image-specific exclusions it has no opinion on.
#
# This is `.dockerignore`, not `.gitignore`: a file can be committed (so the
# build legitimately needs it) while still being useless to the image, and the
# two lists are kept separate for exactly that reason.

# ---- Version control ----
# `.git` is the single largest thing in many checkouts and nothing in the image
# reads it. Excluded here even though the Dockerfiles do not COPY it, because
# the context is sent before the Dockerfile chooses what to COPY.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# ---- Secrets ----
# These must never reach an image layer. `.env` is already gitignored; this is
# the second lock on the same door, because a `COPY . .` that forgot to exclude
# it would bake the operator token -- a refund-capable credential -- into a
# layer. The rendered image gets its configuration from the environment the
# operator supplies at run time (compose `env_file`), never from a copied file.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
*.pem
*.key

# ---- Python: virtualenv, bytecode, caches, build artifacts ----
# The image builds its own environment from pyproject.toml. A copied `.venv`
# would be a Windows venv inside a Linux image: wrong interpreter, wrong wheels,
# and hundreds of megabytes that shadow nothing useful.
.venv/
venv/
env/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/
.eggs/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
coverage.xml
.coverage
coverage.json
htmlcov/

# ---- Local databases and runtime state ----
# `*.db` is the SQLite file the test suite and local dev produce; `.opspilot-data`
# is where the MCP store writes its working copy. Both are runtime artefacts of
# the host, and the container writes its own to a mounted volume instead.
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
data/
.opspilot-data/

# ---- Frontend ----
# `node_modules` is reinstalled inside the web image from package-lock.json (and
# `npm ci` requires the lockfile, which is NOT excluded). `.next` is the local
# build output; the image makes its own. `web/Dockerfile` is excluded from the
# Python image's context only transitively -- it is tiny, so it is left in and
# simply not copied.
node_modules/
.next/
out/
.turbo/
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# ---- Local working notes and eval run output ----
# Scratch notes and per-run eval output are host artefacts. `evals/datasets/`
# (the fixtures the eval harness reads) is deliberately NOT excluded -- the
# `opspilot-eval` entry point lives in the same image and needs them.
.scratch/
evals/results/*

# ---- Editors / OS ----
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
README.md
docs/

# ---- Docker files themselves ----
# Not needed inside any image, and keeping them out makes a build-context change
# to a Dockerfile not invalidate unrelated layers.
docker-compose.yml
.dockerignore
Dockerfile
web/Dockerfile
